{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-12-discounts-commissions-price-stacks",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Trade Discounts, Successive Discounts, and Commission",
    "description": "Track the amount retained after one or more discounts or commissions and connect the receipt to profit or loss.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Apply single and successive discounts as retained-price multipliers, derive the equivalent single discount, and recover an unknown discount or MP from final receipt. Include trade and cash discount sequences, a commission withheld from a listed/retail price, and combined gain after these deductions. Explain why sequential rates are multiplied rather than added. Distinguish commission as a share of retail price from discount as a reduction in the buyer's price. Verify by applying the sequence forward. Use solved examples 30–35 and matching objective problems."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Trade Discounts, Successive Discounts, and Commission: Track the amount retained after one or more discounts or commissions and connect the receipt to profit or loss. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A discount reduces the marked price. A 20% discount leaves 80% of MP, so a price of ₹1,000 becomes ₹800. Use the retained fraction rather than subtracting the rate from a different base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Successive discounts apply one after another. A 10% discount followed by 20% leaves 0.90×0.80=0.72 of MP, equivalent to a single 28% discount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The two rates are not added: after the first discount, the second is applied to a smaller amount. Adding 10%+20%=30% would overstate the reduction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For p% and q% successive discounts, retained factor=(1−p/100)(1−q/100). Equivalent discount=1−that product.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the final price and one discount are known, reverse the last factor first. A ₹105 receipt after a 12.5% second discount came from a pre-second-discount amount of 105/0.875=₹120.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A commission is a portion paid to an agent or retailer, often calculated on retail price. If commission is 36% of retail price ₹100, the producer receives ₹64.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A commission rate reduced by 24 percentage points from 36% becomes 12%. The producer’s receipt changes to 88% of retail price; distinguish percentage points from a 24% relative reduction.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To infer cost from receipt and a stated producer profit, calculate the producer’s receipt after commission and relate it to CP. Example: receive ₹64 and earn 8.8%, so CP=64/1.088.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A discount and commission may both appear, but they can apply to different parties and bases. Track buyer payment, seller receipt, and intermediary commission separately.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If a retailer pays a trade-discounted wholesale price and offers a cash discount to buyers, calculate each retained amount in sequence before measuring profit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For a desired profit after discounts, find target SP from CP and profit rate; then reverse all discounts to find the required MP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check the order with a base of ₹100. Apply the first reduction, then the next; this makes the decreasing bases visible.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When a flat commission amount is given instead of a rate, subtract it from the relevant retail amount to find net receipt, then calculate profit on CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A final retained factor above 1 can occur if a tax or surcharge is added after discounts. Keep additions and deductions as separate multipliers in their stated order.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Finish by naming whose income is being measured: customer price, retailer receipt, producer receipt, or profit. Commission is not automatically the seller’s profit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Name the cost, receipt, and percentage base. Choose an operation that follows the transaction wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
